\lispPackageChapter{sigma/probability}

The \texttt{sigma/probability} package offers a small set of helpers for
working with probabilities in the unit interval.

\section{Macros}

\lispMacroSubsection{decaying-probability?}

The \texttt{decaying-probability?} macro performs a probabilistic test with
\texttt{probability?}.  When the test succeeds, it multiplies the place
\texttt{probability} by \texttt{factor} (default $1/2$) in place, so that
successive calls become less likely, and returns true.  When the test fails, it
returns \texttt{nil} without changing \texttt{probability}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(decaying-probability? \lispVar{probability} \&optional
  \lispVar{factor})}

\lispArgs

\begin{description}
\item[\lispVar{probability}] A place holding a value of type
  \texttt{probability}.
\item[\lispVar{factor}] The multiplicative decay applied after a successful
  trial.  The default is $1/2$.
\end{description}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(let ((p 1.0))
  (loop while (decaying-probability? p)
        collect p))
;; Collects a geometrically decaying sequence until a trial fails.
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Functions}

\lispFunctionSubsection{probability?}

The \texttt{probability?} function returns true with the given probability.  It
draws a uniform random number in $[0,1)$ and returns true when that number is
less than or equal to \texttt{probability}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(probability? \lispVar{probability})}

\lispArgs

\begin{description}
\item[\lispVar{probability}] A value of type \texttt{probability}.
\end{description}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(probability? 0.0) ; Always returns NIL
(probability? 1.0) ; Always returns T
(probability? 0.5) ; Returns T or NIL with equal chance
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Types}

\lispTypeSubsection{probability}

The \texttt{probability} type comprises values suitable for use as
probabilities: floating-point numbers in $[0.0, 1.0]$, the integers $0$ and $1$,
and bits.
